## Expressive fairy-tale animation language

Wondertown uses the visual language of a classic hand-drawn romantic
fairy-tale feature, presented as a premium children’s storybook. The goal is
not realism. It is clarity, warmth, theatrical staging, and characters whose
faces can genuinely act.

The definitive identity reference for scene illustration is
[wondertown-cast-reference.png](design-explorations/wondertown-cast-reference.png).
Use it to lock each character’s face, silhouette, costume, palette, and
construction before changing expression, pose, camera angle, or lighting.
The characters are displayed at normalized reference sizes on that sheet;
their actual in-world scale still follows the story, including Pip’s teacup
height.

### Character shape language

* Build characters from **simplified, appealing 2D animation shapes** rather
  than realistically modelled anatomy or materials.
* Use elegant clean linework, bold readable silhouettes, clear facial planes,
  and simplified cel-painted colour.
* Design broad, readable eyes and brows; elastic cheeks and mouths; expressive
  noses, muzzles, jaws, ears, and head tilts; and hands large enough to carry
  gesture.
* A face must extrapolate naturally into joy, fear, suspicion, sadness, anger,
  embarrassment, affection, and determination without changing the model.
* Let posture carry emotion: compressed shapes for fear and loneliness,
  stretched shapes for courage and excitement, strong curves for tenderness,
  and straights for stiffness or authority.
* Keep details selective. Costume, seams, patches, wood joints, and clock parts
  clarify identity; they must not bury the face or silhouette.
* Poses should be theatrical and understandable at thumbnail size. Avoid stiff
  model-sheet neutrality in finished scenes.
* Maintain the selected model’s proportions, face construction, outline weight,
  eye language, costume colours, and material cues on every page.

Characters use crisp controlled contours, flat local colours, and one or two
hard-edged cel-shadow families. They must remain cleanly readable against the
background, but lighting colour, cast shadows, edge accents, and atmospheric
overlap should place them inside the scene rather than making them look like
stickers.

### Pip is a tiny workshop folk-child

Pip is **not a wind-up boy, robot, automaton, or toy**. He is a tiny magical
workshop folk-child: brownie- or gnome-like, warm, nimble, curious, and at home
among old tools and toys. His smallness is magical, not mechanical.

Use character design option 2: a soft compact child silhouette, brown padded
work cap, tousled brown hair, warm expressive face, cream rolled-sleeve shirt,
brown leather workshop apron with ordinary hand tools, dark green cropped
trousers, striped socks, sturdy brown boots, and a muted teal neckerchief.

Never draw a winding key, gears, springs, exposed clockwork, mechanical joints,
clock emblems, metal body panels, or a mechanism beneath his clothes. Pip’s
body is that of a living magical child. The clockwork belongs to the workshop
and its toys.

Pip is no taller than a teacup, approximately 8–12 cm in-world. In workshop
scenes he must read as genuinely tiny beside the workbench, drawers, tools,
oil can, broom, door, and floorboards. Use low eye-level perspective and large
foreground forms to preserve this scale.

## Animated-feature environments

Backgrounds use polished digital animation painting that supports the simpler
characters:

* Graceful simplified architecture with exaggerated theatrical proportions
* Strong perspective and large foreground forms framing the composition
* Rounded, stylized geometry rather than realistic construction
* Controlled palettes with warm golden light against cool blue-green shadows
* Broad tonal masses, softened atmospheric distance, and selective crisp edges
* Tactile but simplified wood, wool, leather, brass, glass, snow, and sawdust
* Enough depth and texture to feel magical, never enough to compete with faces

Match light direction, warm/cool colour, cast shadows, and atmosphere across
characters and environment. Environments may use softer gradients and deeper
atmosphere than characters, while character faces and silhouettes stay crisp.

## Reusable whole-book prompt

> Premium children’s storybook illustration in classic hand-drawn romantic
> fairy-tale animation language. Expressive 2D characters built from simplified
> appealing shapes, elegant clean linework, bold readable silhouettes, broad
> eyes and brows, elastic cheeks and mouths, gesture-rich poses, flat local
> colour, and restrained hard-edged cel shading. Polished theatrical digital
> backgrounds with graceful exaggerated architecture, warm golden highlights,
> cool blue-green shadows, soft atmospheric depth, and carefully planned
> composition. Faces must support a wide range of extrapolated expressions.
> Warm, magical, inviting, and emotionally legible for children.

## Character prompt block

> Expressive hand-drawn fairy-tale animation character with simplified 2D
> shapes, clean controlled contours, strong silhouette, broad readable eyes and
> brows, elastic cheeks and mouth, clear facial planes, gestural hands and pose,
> flat local colours, and one or two restrained hard-edged cel-shadow shapes.
> The face must be capable of joy, fear, suspicion, sadness, anger,
> embarrassment, affection, and determination while staying on model.

## Negative direction

> No photorealism, realistic anatomy, realistic material rendering, glossy CGI,
> plastic 3D surfaces, stiff doll faces, anime styling, painterly character
> modelling, airbrushed character gradients, comic-book cross-hatching, heavy
> black outlines on environments, concept-art sketchiness, excessive
> micro-detail, modern flat vector graphics, or random decorative clutter. Pip
> must never have a winding key, gears, springs, mechanical joints, clockwork
> anatomy, or robotic features.

The compact style label is:

**“Expressive classic fairy-tale 2D animation characters in theatrical digital
storybook backgrounds.”**

## Integrated text-and-illustration layouts

Pages that need story text use **planned negative space** (also called
**copy space**) as part of the original composition. The preferred term for
this book is **integrated negative space with overlapping illustration**:

* Reserve only the needed area—normally **20–25% of the image**—for text.
  It must never continue below the foreground table, ground plane, or other
  depth boundary merely because that would leave more empty colour.
* The text space is part of the scene rather than a box or a side panel. Use
  a quiet, low-contrast, out-of-focus **neutral sepia-green** depth field;
  it should be noticeably softer and darker than white, while still supporting
  readable dark text.
* Place the field behind selected foreground objects. Characters, furniture,
  branches, props, or scenery may overlap, frame, and intrude into it with
  crisp silhouettes—like an interesting mask cut from the text field.
* Keep the foreground edges sharp where they meet this field. Do not paint a
  foggy blur over the objects or dissolve their contours. The depth field is
  behind the objects, as if the background were z-clipped directly behind
  them.
* The text may use contour wrapping around the foreground silhouette. In
  comic terminology, an object crossing the quiet field is a **panel
  breakout**, **border break**, or **breaking the panel border**; it is not
  “breaking the fourth wall.”
* Keep the bottom-right area free of text space and visual obstruction for
  in-game actions.

Reusable composition prompt:

> Children’s-book page composition with intentionally reserved negative space
> for text, integrated into the illustration; crisp foreground characters and
> objects partially overlap and frame a restrained sepia-green depth field,
> similar to a comic panel border break. The field occupies only the upper
> 20–25% of the page and ends at the foreground depth boundary; no empty space
> below the table or ground plane.
